mel: Anyone seen this in the .NET sampleapp? request ids are getting nuked when auth retry kicks in. traces split and correlation is toast. :confused:

anya_dotnet: Which version and flow? dotnet sampleapp or internal SDK?

mel: dotnet-sample v0.3.1, Windows dev box and staging. flow: incoming request has X-Request-ID, token expired -> auth filter does refresh + retry via HttpClient.SendAsync -> downstream logs show no x-request-id or sometimes a single field `HEADERS_REDACTED`.

kevin_obs: `HEADERS_REDACTED` sounds like the observability redaction helper collapsed the header map when it saw a sensitive header. we've seen that previously, but I thought we changed it. can you paste a sanitized log snippet?

mel: pasted below (sanitized):
```
INFO auth: incoming headers {"Authorization":"Bearer ***","X-Request-ID":"cid-44afe"}
WARN auth: token expired; refreshing
DEBUG http: retry outgoing headers HEADERS_REDACTED
INFO proxy: downstream processed without req id
```

sam_chang: two possible causes: 1) HttpClient retry wrapper isn't copying request.Headers when building retry message; 2) the redactor in the logging pipeline returns a single REDACTED placeholder instead of a keyed map when it finds Authorization. which layer produced HEADERS_REDACTED? agent or sampleapp?

anya_dotnet: i instrumented locally: HttpClient in our auth filter clones the original HttpRequestMessage but we were using `req.Headers.ToString()` to log headers. That serializes into a single string. the redactor runs on serialized strings and because it matches the Authorization token, it replaces the entire string with HEADERS_REDACTED. so two bugs stack: serializing headers as a blob + redactor that replaces the whole blob instead of redacting a single value.

kevin_obs: ah. so root causes: bad logging practice (stringifying header collection) and redactor behavior. fix options: - change sampleapp to log headers as key/value map (preserve keys) and avoid string concat; - change redactor to operate on header maps when possible, masking values per-key and preserving keys; - add a defensive middleware that ensures X-Request-ID is explicitly propagated into outgoing requests on retries.

mel: nailed it. quick local patch: in auth filter we now use `foreach (var h in req.Headers) logger.Debug("hdr {Key}: {Value}", h.Key, string.Join(",", h.Value));` instead of `Headers.ToString()`. that prevents the redactor from collapsing everything. also added an HttpClient DelegatingHandler to inject X-Request-ID into outgoing retries:
```csharp
public class ReqIdHandler : DelegatingHandler {
  protected override Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(HttpRequestMessage req, CancellationToken ct) {
    if (!req.Headers.Contains("X-Request-ID")) {
      var id = RequestContext.Current?.GetRequestId();
      if (id != null) req.Headers.Add("X-Request-ID", id);
    }
    return base.SendAsync(req, ct);
  }
}
```

kevin_obs: good short-term fix. i'll open an observability infra PR to change redactor behavior: when input is a serialized header string that looks like a map, try to parse it and redact values per-key; if parsing fails, fall back to per-key heuristics. unit tests to add: header map with Authorization should become `{Authorization:"REDACTED", X-Request-ID:"cid-..."}` not `HEADERS_REDACTED`.

sam_chang: also add an SDK-level guidance patch for dotnet: recommend using DelegatingHandler for propagation and avoid serializing header maps. put the sample change into dotnet-sample template. add an integration test in telemetry CI that runs auth-refresh and asserts identical X-Request-ID value across initial request, retry request headers, logs, and span attributes.

mel: will open PR for sampleapp and add integration test. adding a temporary env toggle in staging: `FORCE_REQID_HANDLER=true` to enable handler at runtime until templates roll. i'll paste PR links here once created. :thumbsup:

